---
source: system/tests/help.rs
expression: "help_output(\"prlimit\")"
---
Get and set process resource limits

Usage: prlimit [options] [--<resource>=<limit>] [-p PID]
       prlimit [options] [--<resource>=<limit>] COMMAND [args...]

Arguments:
  [COMMAND]...  Command and arguments to run with new limits applied

Options:
  -p, --pid <pid>             Process ID
  -o, --output <OUTPUT>       Define which output columns to use
      --noheadings            Don't print headings
      --raw                   Use the raw output format
  -c, --core[=<limit>]        Max size of core files (bytes)
  -d, --data[=<limit>]        Max size of a process's data segment (bytes)
  -e, --nice[=<limit>]        Max nice priority allowed to raise
  -f, --fsize[=<limit>]       Max size of files written by the process (bytes)
  -i, --sigpending[=<limit>]  Max number of pending signals
  -l, --memlock[=<limit>]     Max locked-in-memory address space (bytes)
  -m, --rss[=<limit>]         Max resident set size (bytes)
  -n, --nofile[=<limit>]      Max number of open files
  -q, --msgqueue[=<limit>]    Max bytes in POSIX message queues (bytes)
  -r, --rtprio[=<limit>]      Max real-time scheduling priority
  -s, --stack[=<limit>]       Max stack size (bytes)
  -t, --cpu[=<limit>]         Max CPU time (seconds)
  -u, --nproc[=<limit>]       Max number of user processes
  -v, --as[=<limit>]          Max virtual memory size (bytes)
  -x, --locks[=<limit>]       Max number of file locks held
  -y, --rttime[=<limit>]      CPU time for real-time tasks (microseconds)
  -h, --help                  Print help
